  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\) of \\(10\\) bananas are worth as much as \\(8\\) oranges. How many oranges are worth as much as \\(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\) of \\(5\\) bananas?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Equivalent quantities of fruit scale by the same factor. The given quantity of bananas is \\(\\frac23\\cdot10=\\frac{20}{3}\\), and the requested quantity is \\(\\frac12\\cdot5=\\frac52\\).\n\nLet \\(o\\) be the number of oranges worth as much as the requested bananas. Comparing the banana quantities gives the proportion\n\\[\n\\frac{o}{8}=\\frac{5/2}{20/3}=\\frac52\\cdot\\frac3{20}=\\frac38.\n\\]\nThus \\(o=8\\cdot\\frac38=3\\). The answer is \\(3\\) oranges, choice C.",
